{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2"],"primary":"c2","title":"California residential security deposit cap, return deadline and itemization rules (Civ. Code § 1950.5, as amended by AB 12)","requirement":"A landlord may not demand or receive security exceeding one month's rent in addition to first month's rent, or two months' rent for qualifying small landlords unless the tenant is a service member. Within 21 calendar days after the tenant vacates, the landlord must return the remaining security with an itemized statement of deductions and supporting documents, and must meet the move-in and move-out photograph requirements.","key_value":"Cap: 1 month's rent (2 months for small landlords, except for service-member tenants); return within 21 calendar days after tenant vacates","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2024-07-01","end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.5 (as amended by Assembly Bill 12)","coverage_conditions":"Rental agreements for residential property used as the tenant's dwelling, statewide. The two-month cap applies to landlords who are natural persons or LLCs whose members are all natural persons and who own no more than two residential rental properties with no more than four dwelling units in total. D007 also lists family trusts as qualifying owners. The two-month cap does not apply if the prospective tenant is a service member.","exemptions":"The subdivision (c) cap does not apply to security collected or demanded before 2024-07-01. The initial inspection requirements and certain electronic-return notice requirements do not apply when a tenancy is terminated under Code of Civil Procedure § 1161(2), (3) or (4).","conflict_note":"The effective dates differ. D007 (c1) gives 2024-07-01, which is when AB 12's deposit cap took effect. D025 (c2) gives 2026-01-01, which likely reflects the date of the current amended version of § 1950.5. 2024-07-01 is kept because D025's own exemption text ties the cap to security collected on or after 2024-07-01."}],"dropped":[]}
